{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And I commanded Joshua, etc.</b> Meaning: I commanded him not to fear the nations, as it says nearby (Deuteronomy 3:22): ״לֹא תִּירָאוּם״. And it does not revert to the statement ״עֵינֶיךָ הָרוֹאוֹת״, for with this the language of a command would not be justified." "And the reason he did not place this statement immediately after the account of the fall of Sihon and Og, but wrote it after the giving of their land to the children of Gad and the children of Reuben and their conditions, is that from the very terms of the condition that Moshe stipulated with the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, a person of faint heart might conclude — since he seeks aid from warriors while HaShem is the One who fights, what need is there for the vanguard of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben? Therefore he placed the statement <b>and I commanded Joshua</b> etc. right after the vanguard of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, to say: do not think that you need the might of those who gird for war, and let fear not enter your heart, for you do not need the battle — and that is what he concludes: <b>for HaShem your Elohim, He is the one who fights for you.</b> He was precise in saying <b>He</b> — to the exclusion of the one who fears, implied by the commandment of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben: ״חֲלוּצִים תַּעַבְרוּ״. As for the reason the vanguard of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben was necessary, I have explained in parashat Mattot; and see nearby at the verse ״אֵת כָּל״." "<b>At that time, saying.</b> The reason he said <b>at that time</b> — meaning, at the time when he saw the condition that I stipulated with the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, the faint-hearted person would be aroused, in the manner I explained in the preceding verse. And his saying <b>saying</b> — it is possible that he did not say to him at that time this exact language; therefore it says ״לֵאמֹר״, meaning: the intent of the statement is this. It further intends that he commanded him to say to Israel, at the time they would enter the war against the seven nations, to remove timidity from their hearts." "<b>All that He did, etc. — so will He do, etc.</b> In this statement he included the reason for his commandment to the children of Gad and the children of Reuben to cross with Israel over the Jordan: for just as HaShem did to the two kings of the Amorites, so will He do to all the kingdoms, etc.; and just as in the defeat of the two Amorite kings all twelve tribes of Israel were together, likewise across the Jordan they will all be gathered, armed, before HaShem. And for this reason Moshe commanded that the children of Gad and the children of Reuben pass over armed for the war of mitzvah — but in truth HaShem is the One who fights, not the armed vanguard. From this it follows that Moshe could not say to Yehoshua ״your eyes have seen״ etc. except after he had stipulated with the children of Gad and the children of Reuben that they pass over armed; without this, what he saw would constitute no proof, for the merit of twelve tribes is not comparable to the merit of nine and a half. And this is what I myself explained in the verse (Numbers 32:6) ״וְאַתֶּם תֵּשְׁבוּ פֹה״ that he said to the children of Gad and Reuben."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:45.490437751Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}